# Architecture & Project Structure

## Layered Architecture
Enforce strict separation of concerns across three layers:
- **Presentation** — `components/`, `app/**/page.tsx` (UI only, no business logic)
- **Application** — `lib/hooks/`, `lib/store/` (orchestrates data + state)
- **Infrastructure** — `app/api/**/route.ts`, `lib/supabase/` (I/O, external services)

## Directory Conventions
```
components/<feature>/          # Feature-scoped UI components
lib/hooks/use<Feature>.ts      # Custom hooks (one concern per hook)
lib/store/<feature>Store.ts    # Zustand stores (one domain per store)
lib/supabase/                  # DB clients: client | server | admin
app/api/<resource>/route.ts    # REST API routes (resource-per-file)
types/<domain>.ts              # Shared TypeScript types per domain
```

## Golden Rules
- Pages fetch data via hooks or server components — never call Supabase directly in a page component.
- API routes handle I/O only — validate input, call a service/lib function, return response.
- Never import `lib/supabase/server` in client components; use `lib/supabase/client` only.
- Co-locate types with the feature they belong to; promote to `types/` only when shared across 3+ features.
- Prefer composition over inheritance. Build small, single-responsibility units.

## Code Quality & Optimisation
- **No dead code** — never leave commented-out blocks, unused imports, or unreachable branches.
- **DRY** — if logic appears twice, extract it. A utility function, a hook, or a shared component.
- **No magic numbers/strings** — extract to a named constant: `MAX_BIO_LENGTH = 300` not `slice(0, 300)`.
- **Keep files lean** — if a file exceeds ~250 lines, it is doing too much; split by concern.
- **No over-engineering** — do not add abstraction layers that have no current consumer. YAGNI.
```typescript
// ❌ BAD — inline logic repeated, magic number
if (bio.length > 300) { ... }
if (username.length > 300) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — extracted, named, reusable
const MAX_FIELD_LENGTH = 300;
const isTooLong = (value: string) => value.length > MAX_FIELD_LENGTH;
```
